dual-buffer, flex layers and auto zones for the llama.cpp pin (b11351)

--dual-buffer / LLAMA_ARG_DUAL_BUFFER (and --dual-buffer-pattern, --dual-buffer-limit):
repacked CPU weights are also kept in the host buffer (original format), with an automatic
RAM guard (cgroup aware, placement based estimate).
--flex-layers N / LLAMA_ARG_FLEX_LAYERS: the first N GPU layers can move between GPU and CPU
at runtime (zone 2: repacked copy + host copy; debug trigger: LLAMA_FLEX_TEST).
--auto-zones / LLAMA_ARG_AUTO_ZONES (and --zones-ctx-init / LLAMA_ARG_ZONES_CTX_INIT):
three-zone placement from two --fit runs (dense and MoE), a KV cache that grows with the use
(growable VMM buffers on CUDA/HIP, copy fallback elsewhere; LLAMA_KV_NO_VMM=1 turns VMM off),
and flex layers that go off when the KV needs device memory and come back when it shrinks.
VMM layout: one address range per device, a section per K/V, fixed size chunks (chosen at load from the granularity, the alignment and the measured driver booking unit; GGML_CUDA_VMM_CHUNK_KIB), shrunk in place (on HIP the arena is freed via the host: unmapped chunks keep their VRAM),
after mapping a KFD barrier on HIP (GGML_CUDA_VMM_SYNC; a failed barrier falls back to a copy), then synchronous
zeroing; growth steps of one chunk per section; llama_memory_reserve grows the KV for a whole prompt in one step (called by llama-server). GGML_CUDA_VMM_DEBUG=1 logs every reservation, mapping,
access setting, zeroing, growth and release.
Ported from csaki89/llama.cpp (dual-buffer branch, 9a6ae470d, based on e14a77971) to the
b11351 pin. Must be applied after 001 and 002 (they touch common.cpp/.h, llama.h and
llama-context.cpp as well).

